>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `SPLIT_TAC`

``` hol4
splitLib.SPLIT_TAC : thm list -> tactic
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Performs one rule-directed split, preferring the conclusion to the
assumptions.

A conclusion split rule normally has the schematic form

``` hol4
|- !P ... . P (c a1 ... an) = branch_formula
```

where `P` is a universally quantified, Boolean-valued predicate and `c`
is a constant. The theorem must have no hypotheses. A rule whose
right-hand side is syntactically a negation is instead classified as an
*assumption split rule*:

``` hol4
|- !P ... . P (c a1 ... an) = ~branch_formula
```

The distinction is structural; it is not inferred from the logical
meaning of the theorem.

`SPLIT_TAC rules` first searches the conclusion using the conclusion
rules. It selects one matching constant-headed redex, instantiates `P`
with its surrounding Boolean context, and rewrites that context with the
rule. All alpha-equivalent occurrences of the selected redex in that
context are replaced. Candidate occurrences are ordered by the required
enclosing-binder depth, then by term depth and left-to-right position.

If the conclusion cannot be changed, the tactic searches the assumptions
using the assumption rules. It chooses the first assumption containing a
key constant, replaces that assumption by the facts obtained from the
instantiated rule, and may thereby create multiple goals. In particular,
a merely syntactic occurrence of a key in an earlier assumption can
prevent a later assumption from being tried if the former cannot
actually be split.

Exactly one rule application is performed. Repetition, when wanted, must
be supplied by a surrounding tactic or by the simplifier's looper
machinery.

### The `[split]` attribute

A stored theorem can be registered in the persistent split theorem set
by attaching the argument-free `split` attribute:

``` hol4
Theorem pick_split[split]:
  !P. P (pick b x y) <=>
      (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
Proof
  ...
QED
```

Registration checks the rule format described above. Stored registrations
are identified by their full database names, such as
`myTheory$pick_split`, and are inherited through theory ancestry. The
[`simpLib.split_ss`](#simpLib.split_ss) fragment consults this set at run
time. `SPLIT_TAC` itself does not: it uses only its explicit theorem
list.

Within a simplifier invocation, a registered conclusion rule is named
`split myTheory$pick_split`; an assumption rule is named
`split_asm myTheory$pick_split`. These names can be passed to `Excl` to
suppress an individual rule for that invocation.

### Failure

Constructing the tactic fails if any supplied theorem is malformed. A
rule is malformed if it has hypotheses, its conclusion is not an
equality, its left side is not `P (c a1 ... an)`, `P` is not a
universally quantified Boolean-valued variable of the right type, or the
redex is not constant-headed.

Application fails if no conclusion rule changes the conclusion and no
assumption rule can split the selected assumption. Instantiation errors
from one conclusion candidate are skipped in favour of later candidates.

### Example

The following is a static illustration of one conclusion-splitting
step:

``` hol4
val bool_split = splitLib.type_split_of ``:bool``;

(* Goal before:  P (if b then x else y)

   splitLib.SPLIT_TAC [bool_split]

   Goal after:   (b ==> P x) /\ (~b ==> P y)
*)
```

### Comparison with global case splitting

`SPLIT_TAC` rewrites one case-like redex inside a Boolean context. To
case-split a chosen scrutinee globally across the whole goal, use
[`bossLib.Cases_on`](#bossLib.Cases_on). To find a `case` expression,
case-split its scrutinee globally, and simplify the resulting goals, use
[`BasicProvers.CASE_TAC`](#BasicProvers.CASE_TAC).

There is one unrelated local shadow of this name in the examples tree:
`examples/machine-code/hoare-triple/set_sepScript.sml:60` defines a local
`SPLIT_TAC` as a simplification-and-METIS tactic. Qualify the library
operation as `splitLib.SPLIT_TAC` in that scope.

